'use strict';
const {course}=require('./data-wave2-helper');
const specs=[
{id:'dc-missing',comps:['m1.c1'],title:'Classify missingness before choosing a treatment',intro:'A customer export has absent income values. Dropping rows, filling a value and retaining an explicit missing flag answer different questions and can represent different populations.',sections:[['Unknown is a state, not a default number','Use isna/notna to identify pandas missing markers rather than equality with NaN. An empty string, a sentinel such as -999 and a missing value can mean different things; interpret them using the source contract. Missingness may depend on observed or unobserved outcomes, so its pattern matters to analysis.','Dropping incomplete cases can be defensible for a purpose but can change coverage and bias results. Imputation replaces unknowns with an assumption or model; it does not reveal their true values. A missing indicator can preserve information about absence without pretending to measure the missing quantity.'],['Keep treatment tied to the downstream task','For a descriptive report, show observed-denominator coverage and conduct sensitivity checks when missingness could matter. For predictive modeling, fit imputation rules on training data only and apply the saved rule to later data. Learnable preprocessing on held-out data can leak evaluation information.','The fixture keeps raw values, adds a missing flag and creates an illustrative median-filled view. It reports how many values were filled. This is a demonstration of mechanics, not a recommendation that median imputation is appropriate for every income dataset.']],problem:'Preserve a raw Series while building an explicitly flagged imputed view.',steps:['Identify the missing position with isna.','Calculate the median of observed fixture values.','Fill a new view and report the number of assumptions introduced.'],code:'import pandas as pd\nraw=pd.Series([10.,None,30.])\nflag=raw.isna()\nfilled=raw.fillna(raw.median())\nprint(flag.tolist())\nprint(filled.tolist())\nprint(int(raw.isna().sum()),int(filled.isna().sum()))',expect:'[False, True, False]\n[10.0, 20.0, 30.0]\n1 0',limit:'The filled value is an explicit assumption, not recovered truth. Its suitability, selection effects and modeling consequences need purpose-specific review.',mistakes:['NaN equality is not the correct general missingness test.','A filled column can look complete while retaining substantial uncertainty.'],takeaways:['Profile missingness and its meaning.','Preserve raw evidence and label imputation assumptions.'],practice:[
['Which pandas method identifies missing values?','isna','Equality with NaN always','sort_values','duplicated only','isna handles supported missing markers; NaN equality does not behave like ordinary value equality.'],
['Does imputation recover an unknown true value automatically?','No','Yes by definition','Only for medians','Only for integers','Imputation supplies an assumption or prediction, not direct observation of the missing fact.'],
['What does dropping incomplete rows change?','The analyzed population','Only file formatting','Only column names','Nothing substantive','Excluded cases no longer contribute, which can alter representation and estimates.'],
['Income is missing more often for a particular group. What should be examined?','Missingness patterns','Only the final mean','Only the spreadsheet font','Only row order','Systematic missingness can make complete-case analysis unrepresentative of the target population.'],
['A predictive pipeline computes fill medians on final-test data too. What is a concern?','Evaluation leakage','Guaranteed better generalization','Only dtype memory','Only the column label','Fitted preprocessing should not use held-out information intended for independent evaluation.'],
['A report fills all unknown sales with zero. What must justify that choice?','The source/metric meaning','Only code convenience','Only lower totals','Only a faster query','Zero is an observed substantive amount and cannot automatically replace unknown sales.'],
['Why retain an imputation flag?','To expose assumed values','To prove the fill was true','To remove all bias','To identify every cause','A flag distinguishes observed and replaced values for review, modeling or sensitivity checks.']
],formal:[
['What does a missing marker represent under a data contract?','An unavailable/unknown state','A universal numeric zero','A guaranteed record error','A fixed median value','Its precise meaning comes from the collection contract, but it is not automatically a measured amount.'],
['Which choice can preserve explicit absence information?','A missingness indicator','Deleting all incomplete records silently','Replacing raw evidence permanently','Pretending every fill was observed','An indicator records which measurements were unavailable while a separate treatment can be evaluated.'],
['Is one missing-data policy correct for every task?','No','Yes, always fill zero','Yes, always drop rows','Only if the file is small','Appropriate handling depends on source meaning, missingness mechanism, target population and downstream use.'],
['A clinic drops patients with absent follow-up and reports recovery. What should be investigated?','Outcome-linked attrition','Only date string length','Only the report filename','Only column ordering','Missing follow-up may depend on health outcomes and change the population represented by the observed rate.'],
['A model fills missing age using the full dataset before splitting. What should be changed?','Fit preprocessing on training only','Only rename the filled column','Only round the age','Only shuffle rows after filling','Using evaluation observations to estimate preprocessing parameters leaks held-out information.'],
['A vendor uses -999 to mean unknown temperature. What must precede averaging?','Interpret the sentinel contract','Treat -999 as physical truth','Drop every negative value','Convert all values to strings','A sentinel may need conversion to a missing state under the source definition rather than inclusion as a measured temperature.'],
['A dashboard has no nulls after filling every column. What remains unproved?','Factual completeness/accuracy','That the fill operation ran','That values are represented','That a column exists','Replacing unknowns does not create observed facts or validate the imputation assumptions.'],
['A customer group has systematically missing spending. Which simple assumption is risky?','Complete-case validity','That some rows are absent','That isna can count nulls','That source context matters','Complete-case representativeness needs justification when missingness differs across relevant groups.'],
['An analyst tests several plausible fills and conclusions change sharply. What should be reported?','Sensitivity to assumptions','Only the preferred result','Only the largest sample','Only the cleanest chart','A decision that depends on unobserved values should disclose that assumption sensitivity.'],
['A team overwrites the only raw copy with imputed data. What has been impaired?','Auditability of original evidence','The ability to display numbers','The existence of a DataFrame','The definition of a median','Preserving raw inputs and transformation records allows assumptions and corrections to be reviewed or reversed.']
]},
{id:'dc-invalid',comps:['m1.c2'],title:'Separate impossible values from rare valid observations',intro:'An inventory feed contains negative physical mass and a very large valid bulk shipment. Both deserve attention, but only one violates the stated physical rule.',sections:[['Use domain constraints before statistical shortcuts','Invalidity depends on meaning: a mass cannot be negative under a nonnegative physical-mass contract, but a negative account balance can be valid. Set ranges, allowed states and cross-field rules with domain owners rather than applying a generic positive-number rule to every field.','An outlier is unusual relative to a reference group or model. It may be a genuine incident or valuable rare event. Compare like units and relevant segments; a large shipment is not unusual under a bulk contract even if it is unusual for individual parcels.'],['Flag and investigate without inventing repairs','Create separate flags for contract violations and statistical unusualness. Investigate collection errors, unit changes, duplicate ingestion and real operational events. Corrections need traceable evidence; quarantining uncertain records may be appropriate when downstream safety requires it.','The example marks negative mass invalid and separately flags a review threshold for unusually large mass. A threshold is supplied for the fixture, not estimated as a universal outlier rule. Flagging does not authorize deletion, fraud labeling or automatic replacement.']],problem:'Create distinct invalidity and review flags on a synthetic mass column.',steps:['State the nonnegative physical-mass rule.','Flag impossible negatives separately.','Apply an explicit large-value review threshold without changing values.'],code:'import pandas as pd\nmass=pd.Series([1.,-2.,100.])\ninvalid=mass.lt(0)\nunusual=mass.gt(50)&~invalid\nprint(invalid.tolist())\nprint(unusual.tolist())\nprint(mass.tolist())',expect:'[False, True, False]\n[False, False, True]\n[1.0, -2.0, 100.0]',limit:'The rules only classify this fixture under declared assumptions. A 100kg shipment can be legitimate, and no correction is performed automatically.',mistakes:['A statistical extreme is not proof of an incorrect measurement.','Domain rules cannot be transferred blindly between different fields.'],takeaways:['Distinguish invalid, unusual and unresolved.','Review context and preserve raw values.'],practice:[
['What makes a value impossible?','Violation of a valid domain rule','Distance from the mean alone','Being the largest observation','Having many digits','A domain constraint defines what values cannot represent the intended quantity.'],
['Can an unusual shipment be valid?','Yes','No universally','Only if it is missing','Only if rounded','Rare bulk or incident cases can be genuine observations requiring interpretation rather than deletion.'],
['Should every negative number be removed?','No','Yes for every dataset','Only if stored as float','Only in large files','Some quantities such as balances legitimately take negative values; rules depend on meaning.'],
['A scale feed changes kilograms to grams. What may apparent outliers indicate?','A unit inconsistency','Guaranteed physical incidents','A broken sum function','A normal missing-value policy','A change in numerical scale can create false extremes without a corresponding real-world change.'],
['A large transaction is labeled fraud solely from an outlier flag. What is unsupported?','The intent/conduct conclusion','That a rule flagged it','That an amount exists','That a threshold was applied','Unusualness alone is not evidence sufficient to establish fraud.'],
['A cross-field check finds delivered_at before ordered_at under the same clock contract. What needs review?','Temporal consistency','Only the column sort','Only chart formatting','Only id length','The stated event ordering is violated and needs source/time interpretation checks.'],
['An analyst replaces every extreme with a preferred mean. What is missing?','A justified repair rule','Only a faster expression','Only a new dtype','Only a report title','Automatic replacement can erase valid tail behavior and fabricate values without evidence.']
],formal:[
['Which rule is appropriate for a declared physical mass?','Mass must be nonnegative','Every number must be positive','All large values are false','Every zero is missing','The nonnegative rule follows the stated physical measurement meaning, including a possible zero.'],
['What should an outlier flag trigger?','Contextual review','Automatic fraud conviction','Universal deletion','Guaranteed zero imputation','A flag identifies a case to investigate under the domain and source context.'],
['What is a cross-field validation rule?','A field relationship','Only a dtype label','Only a chart setting','A generic row count','Cross-field rules compare values jointly, such as end time not preceding start time under the clock contract.'],
['A finance feed has a valid negative balance. What would a generic positive-only cleaner do?','Erase meaningful data','Guarantee correction','Improve every metric','Recover the true balance','A rule borrowed from unrelated physical quantities would misclassify a legitimate financial state.'],
['A hospital’s rare long stay is removed to stabilize an average. What should be considered?','Whether it is valid','Only the lower final average','Only the patient-id type','Only file size','Rare valid stays can be central to resource burden and patient outcomes.'],
['A device reports a value beyond its documented sensor range. What is warranted?','A source/domain investigation','A guaranteed replacement median','A universal causal explanation','A silent record deletion','The range violation merits review, but the correct repair requires evidence about the collection failure.'],
['Anomaly thresholds are trained on individual parcels and applied to freight pallets. What is wrong?','The reference population','The ability to compare values','Only the column name','Only numeric precision','Different business segments can have different legitimate scales and distributions.'],
['A time ordering violation appears after combining timezones. What should be checked?','Timezone and event semantics','Only a shorter date string','Only row position','Only output color','Apparent temporal impossibility can result from inconsistent timezone interpretation rather than real sequence.'],
['A record is unusual and valid. What can cleaning appropriately do?','Retain it with review metadata','Change it until ordinary','Delete it without a trace','Treat it as missing automatically','Valid rare evidence can be preserved while its unusualness is documented for downstream analysis.'],
['A team wants one universal numeric-outlier cutoff for all fields. What limits that approach?','Different units and distributions','All numbers share one meaning','Only chart orientation','Only filesystem permissions','Appropriate unusualness rules depend on units, population, domain and the consequences of errors.']
]},
{id:'dc-strategy',comps:['m1.c1','m1.c2'],title:'Compare cleaning strategies with coverage and sensitivity evidence',intro:'A cleaning decision can improve format consistency while changing the answer to the business question. Evaluate what each treatment excludes or assumes before accepting a tidy result.',sections:[['Measure the effect of the policy','For each rule, record affected rows, eligible population and fields changed. Compare output counts and key measures with the raw baseline. Dropping a small random fraction differs from excluding every user in one region; impact needs both totals and segment coverage.','An imputation can shrink variation and create artificial relationships. Deleting extremes can remove the losses or delays the decision aims to understand. Use sensitivity comparisons among plausible policies when unknown values materially affect an outcome, and distinguish an exploratory comparison from an approved production rule.'],['Use quarantine when confidence is insufficient','Some records cannot be safely corrected from available evidence. Keep them separate with reason codes and preserve original identity so data owners can investigate. Quarantine should not become an invisible denominator change; reports must disclose excluded coverage when relevant.','The fixture compares an observed-only mean with two explicit fill assumptions and counts the unknown cases. It makes assumption sensitivity visible rather than choosing the most attractive answer. The values are synthetic and no policy is claimed universally correct.']],problem:'Show how plausible missing-value assumptions change a summary.',steps:['Count unknown values in the raw sample.','Compute the observed-only summary.','Compare two named fill assumptions without overwriting the source.'],code:'import pandas as pd\ns=pd.Series([10.,None,30.])\nprint(int(s.isna().sum()))\nprint(s.mean())\nprint(s.fillna(0).mean(),s.fillna(100).mean())',expect:'1\n20.0\n13.333333333333334 46.666666666666664',limit:'The spread between assumptions illustrates sensitivity, not a probability distribution for the missing value or an approved fill recommendation.',mistakes:['The cleanest-looking output can rest on the strongest unsupported assumptions.','Excluded rows must not disappear from coverage reporting.'],takeaways:['Quantify what cleaning changes.','Expose assumption-sensitive conclusions and unresolved records.'],practice:[
['What should be counted for each cleaning rule?','Affected and retained records','Only final chart bars','Only renamed columns','Only source filename length','Impact counts show how much evidence a rule alters or excludes.'],
['Does a smaller output variance prove an imputation is correct?','No','Yes universally','Only for mean fills','Only in small samples','Filling with common values can artificially shrink variation without recovering true observations.'],
['What is a sensitivity comparison useful for?','Testing dependence on assumptions','Choosing the prettiest result','Proving one unknown value true','Removing every limitation','Different plausible treatments reveal whether conclusions rely strongly on unobserved or uncertain values.'],
['A rule removes every rural customer but few rows overall. What must be visible?','Segment-specific coverage','Only the overall percentage','Only output file size','Only the largest segment','Small global loss can still erase a relevant subgroup and invalidate claims about it.'],
['An unknown record cannot be safely corrected. What is a defensible option?','Quarantine with a reason','Invent a likely value','Delete all traces','Declare it verified','Preserved identities and reason codes support investigation without manufacturing evidence.'],
['A report excludes quarantined outcomes. What should readers know?','The exclusion scope','Only the remaining mean','That all source cases are represented','Only the pipeline name','Quarantine can change the analyzed population and must not hide relevant missing coverage.'],
['The preferred conclusion flips between reasonable fill policies. What is appropriate?','Report assumption sensitivity','Publish only the favored policy','Add more decimal places','Rename the metric','A decision-sensitive uncertainty should be disclosed and may motivate better data collection.']
],formal:[
['What distinguishes a cleaning assumption from an observation?','An assumed value','It is always more accurate','It cannot affect results','It must be numeric','Replacement or exclusion policies add assumptions about unavailable/invalid evidence.'],
['What should quarantine preserve?','Original identity and reason','Only the new average','Only a chart screenshot','A random surrogate outcome','Traceability enables review, correction and coverage accounting for excluded evidence.'],
['Can complete-looking data still be highly uncertain?','Yes','No if null count is zero','Only for strings','Only after sorting','Imputed or inferred values may contain substantial uncertainty despite no remaining nulls.'],
['An analyst selects the fill that maximizes revenue. What is the concern?','Outcome-driven cleaning','Guaranteed better measurement','Only computational speed','A necessary financial rule','Cleaning should be justified by source meaning and purpose, not chosen to manufacture a favorable result.'],
['A validation report lists rejected counts but not which groups were affected. What can be hidden?','Unequal coverage loss','Only memory use','Only column ordering','Only output encoding','Aggregate rejection totals can conceal systematic exclusion of regions, products or customer groups.'],
['A missing-cost fill creates higher margins. What should be checked?','Assumption and impact','Only whether code executed','Only the profit title','Only a larger sample id','A fill value can change conclusions mechanically and needs evidence-based justification.'],
['A cleaner removes all unusually long delays. What target may be undermined?','Measuring service failures','Only faster CSV loading','Only normalizing column names','Only date formatting','Rare delays may be exactly the outcome the analysis needs to quantify.'],
['A team compares output sums with raw sums after an approved correction. Why?','To reconcile transformation impact','To prove no assumptions exist','To remove access controls','To infer causality automatically','Reconciliation makes changes attributable to specific rules and exposes accidental loss or duplication.'],
['A small unknown fraction changes a high-stakes decision. What may be needed?','Better evidence','Only more rounding','Only an optimistic fill','Only a new chart','Even few unknowns can be decisive when outcomes or costs are concentrated; collecting or validating evidence can be necessary.'],
['A cleaning policy is appropriate for a forecast but reused for an audit total. What should be revisited?','The downstream purpose','Only the helper function name','Only output format','Only the data type','Prediction-oriented imputation and accounting/audit evidence can have different acceptable assumptions and requirements.']
]},
{id:'dc-standardize',comps:['m2.c1'],title:'Standardize text, categories and dates with reversible rules',intro:'A regional report contains " North ", "NORTH" and a source abbreviation "N". Some differences are formatting, while others require a domain dictionary; collapsing strings blindly can combine distinct meanings.',sections:[['Normalize only what the identifier permits','Text cleaning may strip surrounding whitespace and apply a case convention, but those transformations are not universally safe for identifiers or case-sensitive codes. Map approved aliases to canonical categories with a reviewed dictionary and retain unmapped values for investigation. Unicode normalization and punctuation removal also need a stated purpose.','Dates need an explicit format, timezone and ambiguity policy. A string such as 01/02/2026 can refer to different dates in different conventions. Parsing with errors="coerce" creates missing dates on failure; report those failures rather than interpreting them as no event.'],['Units and conversions are semantic changes','Convert measurements only when source units are known. Record conversion factors, precision and rounding rules, and retain original values/units for lineage. A unit inferred from a preferred numerical range is a hypothesis requiring evidence, not an automatic repair.','The fixture standardizes a small approved region vocabulary and parses an ISO date under an explicit format. Unknown categories and bad dates remain visible as missing outputs. It does not establish that the same rules suit customer names, security identifiers or all source locales.']],problem:'Apply an approved category map and an explicit date parser.',steps:['Strip and uppercase only the declared region labels.','Map approved aliases while exposing unknowns.','Parse the declared ISO format and count failures.'],code:'import pandas as pd\nraw=pd.Series([" North ","N","mystery"],dtype="string")\ncanon=raw.str.strip().str.upper().map({"NORTH":"north","N":"north"})\ndates=pd.to_datetime(pd.Series(["2026-01-02","bad"]),format="%Y-%m-%d",errors="coerce")\nprint(canon.fillna("UNMAPPED").tolist())\nprint(dates.isna().tolist())',expect:"['north', 'north', 'UNMAPPED']\n[False, True]",limit:'The alias dictionary and format are explicitly approved only for this synthetic example. Unknowns are exposed, not guessed.',mistakes:['Removing punctuation or changing case can corrupt meaningful identifiers.','An ambiguous date string should not be silently interpreted under an unstated locale.'],takeaways:['Use reviewed dictionaries and explicit formats.','Preserve unmatched categories and parse failures.'],practice:[
['What should govern category alias mapping?','An approved dictionary','Only alphabetic sorting','Only the shortest label','Only value frequency','Aliases should reflect shared business meaning rather than arbitrary similarity.'],
['Does uppercasing every identifier preserve meaning universally?','No','Yes by definition','Only for numeric strings','Only in a DataFrame','Some identifiers are case-sensitive and normalization can collapse distinct entities.'],
['What does an explicit date format reduce?','Parsing ambiguity','All missingness','Every source error','All timezone effects','A specified format distinguishes supported representations rather than relying on ambiguous inference.'],
['A date 01/02/2026 arrives without a convention. What is appropriate?','Resolve the source format','Choose the preferred month','Assume every locale agrees','Replace it with today','Month/day ordering is ambiguous and must be resolved from the source contract.'],
['An alias map has no entry for a new region. What should happen?','Flag the unmapped category','Map it to the largest region','Delete the raw text','Assume it means unknown sales','Unmapped values require explicit review rather than invented category membership.'],
['A mass column lacks units but contains large numbers. What is missing before conversion?','Verified source units','Only a division operator','Only numeric dtype','Only the median','Numerical magnitude alone does not prove whether values are grams, kilograms or another unit.'],
['A date parser coerces bad strings to NaT. What should a report retain?','Parse-failure coverage','Only successful dates','A claim of no events','Only new column names','Coercion creates missingness and may exclude records from time-based analysis.']
],formal:[
['What distinguishes standardization from arbitrary rewriting?','A documented rule','Only shorter text','Only fewer categories','Only faster execution','Standardization must preserve intended meaning under a justified convention.'],
['What can str.strip remove in the shown policy?','Boundary whitespace','Every internal word','All identifier differences','All missing values','The operation removes surrounding whitespace but does not establish a universal name/identifier-cleaning policy.'],
['What is NaT used to represent?','A missing datetime','A guaranteed date zero','A successful event','A currency conversion','NaT is pandas’ missing marker for datetime-like values.'],
['A customer-id system distinguishes ab and AB. What would uppercase normalization risk?','Entity collisions','Guaranteed corrected ids','Automatic deduplication accuracy','Only display changes','Case folding can combine identifiers that the source system treats as distinct.'],
['A category dictionary is changed after a reorganization. What should be recorded?','Its version/meaning','Only the report color','Only table width','Only filename length','Mapping changes alter classifications and need traceable version/context for comparisons.'],
['A timestamp is parsed without a timezone then compared across regions. What needs review?','Clock/timezone interpretation','Only chronological sorting','Only the column title','Only the string dtype','Local timestamps can refer to different instants and daylight-saving ambiguities require a policy.'],
['A cleaner divides all masses by 1000 because they look large. What is unsupported?','The inferred conversion','That division can run','That values are numeric','That a column exists','A conversion factor needs evidence of the source/target units, not a preferred output range.'],
['A report silently excludes every unparsed date. What must be assessed?','Time/population coverage loss','Only output formatting','Only the oldest valid date','Only the chart scale','Parse failures can remove specific sources or periods and distort the reported trend.'],
['An alias map turns "other" into the most common category. What is missing?','Evidence of equivalence','Only a shorter dictionary','Only a faster lookup','Only a sorted category list','Convenience or frequency does not establish that two labels share the same domain meaning.'],
['A unit conversion rounds intermediate values too early. What can happen?','Accumulated precision loss','Guaranteed exact totals','Only changed row ids','Automatic source validation','Rounding should follow the business precision policy and avoid unnecessary intermediate loss.']
]},
{id:'dc-dedup',comps:['m2.c2'],title:'Deduplicate by logical identity rather than visual similarity',intro:'An ingestion retry can produce two rows for one order, while two legitimate orders may look identical. Deduplication must begin with the grain and logical identity of the business event.',sections:[['Choose a key from the event contract','duplicated and drop_duplicates can use a subset of columns. A complete-row comparison removes exact repeated rows, but may miss duplicates with ingestion metadata differences. Conversely, matching only customer and amount can merge two real purchases. Define stable event/entity keys with data owners.','keep="first" or keep="last" selects by current row order, not automatically by authority or newest business revision. Sort with a complete deterministic precedence rule only when that rule reflects the intended revision policy. Detect ties rather than allowing incidental file order to choose silently.'],['Distinguish events, snapshots and corrections','For event data, stable event ids can identify retries. For snapshots, one entity may appear legitimately at many dates. For corrections, repeated ids may represent versions that need reconciliation rather than deletion. Preserve discarded evidence and reason codes so decisions remain auditable.','The fixture finds duplicated order ids, then selects the latest declared revision with a deterministic sort. This is valid only under the stated revision-authority assumption; it does not resolve conflicting simultaneous versions or establish that one row per order is right for every source.']],problem:'Select the latest authorized revision by an explicit order key.',steps:['Detect repeated logical order ids.','Sort by the declared increasing revision number.','Keep the last revision and preserve the key/amount relationship.'],code:'import pandas as pd\ndf=pd.DataFrame({"order":["a","a","b"],"revision":[1,2,1],"amount":[10,12,5]})\nprint(int(df.duplicated("order").sum()))\nclean=df.sort_values(["order","revision"]).drop_duplicates("order",keep="last")\nprint(clean.set_index("order")["amount"].to_dict())',expect:"1\n{'a': 12, 'b': 5}",limit:'Revision order is authoritative by fixture declaration. Real equal-version conflicts, missing ids and source precedence need additional validation.',mistakes:['Rows that look similar can be distinct legitimate events.','keep="last" means last in the current order, not necessarily latest or authoritative.'],takeaways:['Define logical identity and revision semantics.','Make deduplication deterministic and traceable.'],practice:[
['What should define deduplication keys?','The logical grain','Only matching amounts','Only row positions','Only the shortest column','Stable business identity distinguishes retries from separate legitimate observations.'],
['What does keep="last" select?','Last row in current order','Always the latest real event','Always the correct amount','The largest value automatically','The selection depends on row order unless a justified precedence ordering is established.'],
['Can identical customer and amount describe two real purchases?','Yes','No universally','Only if values are missing','Only if dates are strings','Different events can share visible attributes, so similarity is not sufficient identity.'],
['A retry adds a new ingestion timestamp but the same event id. Which key can identify the retry?','Stable event id','All columns necessarily','Only the new timestamp','Only amount digits','A stable logical-event identifier can link repeated deliveries despite differing ingestion metadata.'],
['A snapshot has one account per day. Should account id alone remove all repeats?','No, date is part of grain','Yes always','Only if balance is positive','Only if sorted','The same account legitimately appears at multiple snapshot times, requiring a time-aware identity.'],
['Two rows have the same id and revision but conflicting values. What is needed?','Conflict review','A random keep-first rule','Only a new index','Only more decimals','The precedence rule cannot distinguish an equal-version conflict and should not silently choose.'],
['A deduplication removes rows. What should remain available?','Discarded evidence','Only the final mean','Only a green status','Only the newest chart','Traceability supports diagnosis, reconciliation and reversal of incorrect duplicate decisions.']
],formal:[
['Which method can identify duplicate key combinations?','duplicated on keys','mean','astype alone','memory_usage','duplicated evaluates repeated values for the selected identity columns.'],
['Does drop_duplicates infer the correct business key?','No','Yes automatically','Only on small tables','Only with numeric ids','The caller must supply or justify which columns define duplicate identity.'],
['What is a revision-precedence rule?','An authority/version rule','Only row color ordering','A mandatory average','A random tie breaker','The rule defines how repeated logical identities represent updates/corrections and which source/version prevails.'],
['Two identical card charges occur minutes apart and have different authorized transaction ids. What should a cleaner avoid?','Merging them by amount alone','Preserving both ids','Checking source status','Reviewing the event contract','Matching amounts/customers can be coincidental; distinct logical transaction ids may represent real events.'],
['A daily account snapshot is deduplicated by account only. What can be lost?','Legitimate time history','Only extra whitespace','Only file metadata','A guaranteed ingestion error','Entity identity repeats legitimately across dates in snapshot data.'],
['A pipeline keeps the last row without sorting and gets different output after file order changes. What is missing?','Semantic precedence','Only a larger memory limit','Only dtype conversion','Only a renamed output','Incidental source order should not decide authority unless the contract explicitly guarantees it.'],
['An event stream has no reliable id and near-identical rows. What is the honest limitation?','Identity may be unresolved','Duplicates can always be proved','Every similar row is false','All rows must be deleted','Without enough identity evidence, confidently distinguishing retries from separate events may be impossible.'],
['A corrected order has a newer revision and a changed amount. What should be reconciled?','Version and total impact','Only the output filename','Only column widths','Only row count','A version rule changes the selected evidence and potentially totals, which should be traceable.'],
['A source reuses ids each year. What should the dedup key consider?','The id’s scope/year','Only the amount','Only the current row position','Only all text formatting','Identity uniqueness depends on the source’s actual namespace, which may require additional dimensions.'],
['A cleaner discards duplicates permanently with no log. What is compromised?','Audit and recovery','The ability to sum retained data','The existence of columns','The concept of a key','Without preserved evidence and reasons, incorrect exclusions are hard to investigate or reverse.']
]},
{id:'dc-consistency',comps:['m2.c1','m2.c2'],title:'Validate standardized keys before joining or deduplicating',intro:'A normalization rule can make two source keys look equal. That may reveal a harmless formatting difference or create a new collision between distinct entities. Check the mapping before downstream joins.',sections:[['Measure many-to-one normalization explicitly','Keep original and canonical values in separate fields and count how many original forms map to each canonical key. Some alias mappings are intentionally many-to-one, but collisions in unique customer ids can invalidate identity. Review those cases with the authoritative source.','Normalization order matters. Trimming before alias lookup differs from looking up raw text, and deduplicating before canonicalization can leave repeated canonical keys. A versioned pipeline should state both operation order and expected effects.'],['Protect downstream relationship contracts','Before enriching data, check key nulls and uniqueness on the correct side. Before removing duplicate canonical keys, decide whether they represent one entity, multiple entities or unresolved ambiguity. Do not use a merge failure as a reason to suppress a safeguard.','The fixture case-folds a synthetic key under a declared policy and exposes a collision. It does not automatically merge the records or select a winner. This conservative boundary preserves evidence needed to determine whether the source identifier is actually case-insensitive.']],problem:'Detect a normalization collision without discarding either original record.',steps:['Preserve the raw identifiers.','Build a separate canonical representation under the toy rule.','Find repeated canonical identities for review.'],code:'import pandas as pd\ndf=pd.DataFrame({"raw":["A1"," a1 ","B2"]})\ndf["canonical"]=df["raw"].str.strip().str.upper()\nflag=df["canonical"].duplicated(keep=False)\nprint(df.loc[flag,"raw"].tolist())\nprint(df["canonical"].tolist())',expect:"['A1', ' a1 ']\n['A1', 'A1', 'B2']",limit:'The rule creates a visible collision. Whether those records identify one entity depends on the source contract and is intentionally not decided here.',mistakes:['A canonical-key collision is not automatic proof of duplicate business identity.','Changing normalization order can change results.'],takeaways:['Preserve raw-to-canonical lineage.','Review collisions before joins or record removal.'],practice:[
['Why retain raw and canonical keys separately?','To trace normalization','To prove every alias true','To remove all ambiguity','To double the entity count','The mapping lets reviewers inspect what changed and diagnose collisions.'],
['Can normalization create duplicate canonical keys?','Yes','No by definition','Only with missing values','Only after sorting','Different raw forms may collapse under trimming/case/alias rules.'],
['Does a collision prove two records are the same entity?','No','Yes always','Only for uppercase text','Only if counts are equal','The source identity semantics determine whether the collapse is valid or harmful.'],
['A lookup becomes nonunique after key cleaning. What should happen?','Review the collision','Disable merge validation','Choose a random row','Drop every raw key','Normalization may have changed identity relationships and needs investigation before enrichment.'],
['A pipeline deduplicates before trimming keys and retains canonical repeats. What matters?','Transformation order','Only file encoding','Only output title','Only memory size','Operation sequence affects which values are compared and which repetitions remain.'],
['A source says ids are case-sensitive. What does uppercasing risk?','Combining distinct identities','Only cosmetic differences','Guaranteed key repair','Automatic source agreement','A case-sensitive namespace can assign different entities to differently cased keys.'],
['A reviewer sees only canonical keys after a collision. What is impaired?','Source-level diagnosis','The ability to count rows','The column dtype','The existence of a lookup','Without raw forms and provenance, determining whether the normalization was valid is harder.']
],formal:[
['What is a normalization collision?','A many-to-one mapping','Every value remains unchanged','A missing file only','A guaranteed source duplicate','A many-to-one transformation can collapse raw representations that may or may not identify the same entity.'],
['Which check exposes all members of a repeated key group?','duplicated(keep=False)','duplicated(keep="first") alone','Only mean','Only astype','keep=False marks every row involved in a duplicated key rather than leaving one unflagged.'],
['What should govern normalization order?','A versioned contract','Incidental notebook execution','Only alphabetical step names','Only final chart appearance','Order can alter lookups, collisions and exclusions, so it should be explicit and reproducible.'],
['A supplier-id cleanup maps "X-1" and "X1" together without evidence. What is unsupported?','The identity equivalence','That strings can be edited','That a mapping ran','That two raw forms exist','Punctuation can be meaningful in an identifier, so removing it needs a source-semantic justification.'],
['A canonical lookup has two rows for one normalized customer. What downstream risk exists?','Join multiplication/ambiguity','Guaranteed accurate enrichment','Automatic unit conversion','Only a display warning','A nonunique lookup can expand matching transactions or attach conflicting attributes.'],
['A pipeline hides raw values after standardization to save space. What review capability is reduced?','Reconstructing transformations','Only final row counting','Only sorting canonical keys','Only computing a numeric sum','Raw-to-canonical lineage is needed to diagnose incorrect equivalences and reverse rules.'],
['A new normalization version changes which ids collide. What should be compared?','Mapping and metric impact','Only source filename length','Only chart colors','Only the current maximum id','Identity changes can alter deduplication, joins and analytical populations.'],
['A team treats every collision as a retry and deletes extras. What assumption needs evidence?','Canonical-key authority','That rows are stored','That strings can match','That duplicate flags exist','Deletion is justified only when the canonical key truly identifies one logical event/entity under policy.'],
['A cleaned key is null for several records. What should a merge review include?','Null-key policy','Only nonnull matches','Only the longest key','Only report layout','Null keys can affect matching and exclusion; they require an explicit relationship policy.'],
['A standardization helper is reused across unrelated identifier systems. What is required?','Domain compatibility','Only matching column names','Only equal string lengths','Only a shared file format','The same textual operations can preserve one namespace and corrupt another.']
]},
{id:'dc-rules',comps:['m3.c1'],title:'Write validation rules that report actionable failures',intro:'A cleaning pipeline needs a definition of acceptable output. Validation rules should state what is required and expose where the evidence fails, instead of merely returning an unexplained success badge.',sections:[['Translate the contract into independent checks','Check required columns, nullable fields, allowed categories, type/range rules, key uniqueness and cross-field relationships. Separate structural validity from source completeness and factual accuracy. A passed nonnegative check cannot establish that the amount is correct or in the right currency.','Decide which failures stop processing, which quarantine rows and which produce warnings. Failure policy depends on downstream consequences. Include stable record references and reason categories in controlled diagnostics without dumping sensitive full records into public logs.'],['Validate the validator on known cases','Create small fixtures with valid and deliberately invalid synthetic records. Check that each intended rule catches its target and does not reject valid edge cases. A validation framework is not necessary for every simple contract, but explicit checks should be reproducible and maintained as source definitions evolve.','The fixture collects failure categories for missing ids, unsupported status and negative amounts. It preserves the data rather than auto-repairing it. It does not validate real payment permissions, full accounting correctness or all possible schema variations.']],problem:'Collect rule failures from synthetic order records without printing sensitive payloads.',steps:['Define allowed statuses and a nonnegative amount rule.','Evaluate each row and keep only a fixture id plus reason codes.','Check that a valid row remains unflagged.'],code:'import pandas as pd\ndf=pd.DataFrame({"id":["ok","bad"],"status":["paid","mystery"],"amount":[10,-1]})\nfailures=[]\nfor row in df.itertuples(index=False):\n    reasons=[]\n    if row.status not in {"paid","canceled"}: reasons.append("status")\n    if row.amount<0: reasons.append("amount")\n    if reasons: failures.append((row.id,reasons))\nprint(failures)',expect:"[('bad', ['status', 'amount'])]",limit:'The diagnostics identify the stated toy contract failures. They do not expose real records or prove all business facts for passing rows.',mistakes:['A single pass/fail flag can hide which rule failed and how much data was affected.','Validation logs should not become a new sensitive-data leak.'],takeaways:['Tie rules to the contract and failure policy.','Test both rejected cases and valid boundaries.'],practice:[
['What should a validation rule express?','A specific required condition','A preferred business result','Only a chart style','Only row ordering','Rules should encode meaningful constraints rather than force a desired analytical outcome.'],
['Can a positive amount still be factually wrong?','Yes','No under any contract','Only if stored as text','Only when missing','A range check can pass an incorrect magnitude or wrong currency.'],
['Why use synthetic invalid fixtures?','To verify rule detection','To fabricate production records','To guarantee every source truth','To bypass privacy policy','Controlled counterexamples test whether each intended validation actually fails when it should.'],
['A validation failure is logged with full customer credentials. What is wrong?','Diagnostic disclosure','Only the numeric amount','Only the rule name','Only the file timestamp','Logs should expose necessary controlled diagnostics, not raw sensitive data or credentials.'],
['A new category is valid after a policy change but still rejected. What must be updated?','The contract/check','Only the output font','Only the metric title','Only the row index','Validation must evolve with approved source semantics rather than silently reject legitimate new states.'],
['A key uniqueness check is disabled because it finds duplicates. What should occur instead?','Resolve source/grain','Assume every row unique','Rename the key','Only sort values','A failing safeguard reveals a relationship problem; disabling it does not correct the evidence.'],
['One rule failure should halt an audited financial calculation. What should be explicit?','The stop/quarantine policy','Only a warning color','Only a helper function name','Only output compression','Failure severity and handling must follow the consequences and agreed downstream contract.']
],formal:[
['What does a schema validation pass prove?','The tested constraints passed','All source facts are true','Every population is covered','Every estimate is unbiased','Validation is bounded by its declared rules and does not certify all factual or inferential properties.'],
['Which test is useful for a nonnegative rule?','Test the zero boundary','Only test positive examples','Only test random strings','Never test failures','Boundary fixtures help verify the intended inclusive condition and avoid rejecting valid zero values.'],
['What should a validation diagnostic identify?','Rule and safe reference','Every private source field','Only a success slogan','A preferred chart color','Actionable diagnostics connect a failure to a rule and a traceable reference with appropriate disclosure controls.'],
['A team tests a validator only on already-clean data. What is unverified?','Detection of invalid cases','That the test file exists','That a valid sample passed','That code can execute','Passing clean fixtures cannot show that failure paths catch intended contract violations.'],
['A status allowlist is outdated after an approved migration. What can happen?','Valid rows are rejected','Every metric becomes causal','The source loses all rows','All dates become missing','Checks can be wrong if their contract no longer matches the approved domain definitions.'],
['A pipeline continues after a critical currency mismatch. What needs review?','Severity and handling','Only output filename','Only the warning font','Only row sorting','A critical semantic mismatch can invalidate amounts and may require stopping or quarantine under policy.'],
['A validation log includes raw personal notes for every failed row. What should be minimized?','Sensitive diagnostics','Only the rule count','Only the package version','Only the date separator','Debugging needs controlled references/reasons, not unrestricted replication of sensitive records.'],
['A rule checks end_time>=start_time but clocks use different zones. What is required?','A shared time basis','Only a sorted index','Only string comparison','Only a larger integer type','The constraint is meaningful only after timestamps are interpreted on a compatible clock/timezone basis.'],
['A report says zero failures means no missing events. What is unsupported?','Source completeness','That evaluated rows passed','That checks were run','That failures were counted','Rules over observed rows cannot automatically reveal real-world events absent from the source entirely.'],
['A validation rule is changed to permit a known bad batch without review. What principle is violated?','Contract governance','Only code formatting','Only memory reporting','Only row ordering','Rules should follow approved semantics and consequences, not be weakened to manufacture a pass.']
]},
{id:'dc-lineage',comps:['m3.c1'],title:'Record cleaning decisions as reproducible evidence',intro:'A reviewer asks why an order disappeared from a report. A useful cleaning log answers which rule acted, on which source version and with what effect, without requiring the original analyst’s memory.',sections:[['Separate raw, transformed and reviewed states','Retain immutable raw snapshots under access policy, create derived outputs and record transformation versions. A lineage record can include source hash/version, rule version, execution time, counts changed and controlled references to affected records. An execution timestamp alone does not identify the input data.','Document why the rule exists, the assumptions it adds and how exceptions are handled. Approval of a cleaning policy is separate from a script executing without error. When a rule changes, compare affected identities and important measures before accepting the new output.'],['Use impact summaries alongside row diagnostics','A row-level audit trail helps investigate individual changes; aggregate impact summaries show the scope of exclusions, coercions and fills. Both must respect privacy and retention controls. A report may need coverage statements even when detailed logs are restricted to data stewards.','The fixture produces a compact audit record for whitespace standardization and checks that the original values remain available. It is not an enterprise lineage system and does not certify that the normalization is appropriate for every identifier namespace.']],problem:'Log a deterministic text-cleaning rule with an impact count.',steps:['Keep a separate raw Series.','Apply one named transformation to a new Series.','Record changed-count and source/rule identifiers.'],code:'import pandas as pd\nraw=pd.Series([" north ","south"],dtype="string")\nclean=raw.str.strip()\naudit={"source":"synthetic-v1","rule":"trim-region-v1","changed":int(raw.ne(clean).sum()),"rows":len(raw)}\nprint(audit)\nprint(raw.iloc[0])',expect:"{'source': 'synthetic-v1', 'rule': 'trim-region-v1', 'changed': 1, 'rows': 2}\n north ",limit:'The record demonstrates traceable fixture metadata and preserved raw values, not a complete authorization, storage-integrity or retention implementation.',mistakes:['A last-run timestamp is not enough to reconstruct source versions and rules.','A private audit trail should not be published as a raw student/customer API response.'],takeaways:['Make transformations and reasons reviewable.','Track input/rule versions and impact.'],practice:[
['What is needed to reproduce a cleaning result?','Input and rule versions','Only the latest screenshot','Only the analyst’s name','Only the final row count','Reproduction needs exact source identity and the transformations used, not presentation artifacts alone.'],
['Should execution success equal policy approval?','No','Yes automatically','Only for deterministic rules','Only for small data','Code can run correctly while implementing an unjustified or unapproved business assumption.'],
['Why preserve a separate raw state?','To inspect and reverse changes','To duplicate entities intentionally','To guarantee all values true','To remove access policies','Original evidence supports review, correction and comparison of transformation choices.'],
['A changed rule excludes more customers. What should be compared?','Identities and metrics','Only the new version name','Only export size','Only chart order','A policy change can alter coverage and results, so its substantive impact needs review.'],
['A row audit exists but no aggregate impact count. What is harder to assess?','The overall scope of change','Whether a row can be named','Whether source data exists','Whether code ran','Aggregate summaries expose how much data a rule altered and whether particular segments were affected.'],
['An audit file includes sensitive payloads unrelated to the failure. What is needed?','Minimal diagnostics','Only more disk space','Only a new file suffix','Only a larger table','Traceability should use necessary authorized detail and avoid creating another disclosure channel.'],
['A source is overwritten after every refresh. What helps prior-result reconstruction?','Versioned source snapshots','Only final output sorting','Only the current title','Only a shorter script','Reproducibility requires access to the data state used at the time, within retention/access policy.']
],formal:[
['What does lineage connect?','Source-to-output steps','Only chart colors','Only package names','Only human job titles','Lineage traces how a reported output derives from specific evidence and processing steps.'],
['What is an impact count?','How many records a rule changed','Only total CPU time','Only number of chart bars','The length of a function name','Impact counts quantify a rule’s effect on observed records and coverage.'],
['Does a rule version alone identify the input?','No','Yes always','Only for CSV data','Only if hashed twice','The same rule can run on different data; source identity/version must be recorded separately.'],
['A reviewer cannot explain a removed transaction. Which artifact is most useful?','A controlled audit trail','Only the final chart','Only the analyst’s calendar','Only file compression settings','A traceable reason and source/rule reference links the exclusion to its justification.'],
['A new cleaning version changes revenue totals. What should accompany release?','Rule-impact reconciliation','Only a green build badge','Only the new script name','Only a report date','Reviewers need to see which transformations/records explain the change rather than assume it is harmless.'],
['A report’s raw inputs contain personal data. How should lineage access be handled?','Under data controls','Publicly for every viewer','Only by filename secrecy','With no record of access','Traceability does not remove privacy and authorization obligations for underlying records.'],
['An analyst deletes the original invalid text after coercing it. What is lost?','Evidence for source diagnosis','The ability to count current nulls','The name of the output column','The fact that coercion ran','Original representations can be necessary to identify parsing patterns and justify corrections.'],
['A log says rule passed but does not state affected counts. What remains unclear?','The transformation’s scope','Whether the word passed exists','Whether the script had a name','Whether output can be stored','A binary status hides how many records were excluded, replaced or standardized.'],
['Two outputs have the same row count but different entities. What should an audit compare?','Identity-level changes','Only counts again','Only output extension','Only file permissions','Equal size can conceal replacement, exclusion and duplication of different logical records.'],
['A deterministic transformation is documented but violates source meaning. What remains necessary?','Substantive domain review','Only another execution','Only a longer hash','Only more code comments','Reproducibility and determinism do not prove that a rule preserves the intended real-world meaning.']
]},
{id:'dc-idempotence',comps:['m3.c1'],title:'Test reruns, reconciliation and safe pipeline boundaries',intro:'A recurring cleaning job should not create a different population every time it is rerun on the same snapshot. Deterministic inputs, explicit policies and checks make the process recoverable.',sections:[['Idempotence is useful but not sufficient','A transformation is idempotent when applying it twice gives the same result as once. Trimming surrounding whitespace is often idempotent, while multiplying values by a unit factor repeatedly is not unless the pipeline tracks which representation is being converted. Idempotence alone does not mean a rule is correct.','Design reruns against immutable source snapshots or explicit state/version markers. Avoid repeatedly cleaning already-cleaned data without knowing its units, rule versions and provenance. Preserve transactional or atomic output boundaries appropriate to the storage system so failures do not leave a half-published dataset.'],['Test invariants and known counterexamples','Use tests for deterministic output, preserved keys, intended row counts, authorized exclusions, reconciled sums and expected handling of missing/invalid cases. Include edge cases such as all-missing columns, empty batches and equal-version conflicts. Do not weaken tests merely to accept a difficult batch.','The fixture checks one text-standardization rule for idempotence and deliberately shows that repeated scaling is not. This exposes a rerun risk; it does not test filesystem atomicity, distributed scheduling or a production ingestion system.']],problem:'Contrast an idempotent standardization with an unsafe repeated unit conversion.',steps:['Apply the declared trim/case rule twice.','Compare the two transformed Series exactly.','Show repeated scaling changes the already-converted number.'],code:'import pandas as pd\ndef normalize(s): return s.str.strip().str.upper()\nraw=pd.Series([" n ","s"],dtype="string")\nonce=normalize(raw)\nprint(once.equals(normalize(once)))\nx=pd.Series([1000.])\nprint((x/1000).tolist(),((x/1000)/1000).tolist())',expect:'True\n[1.0] [0.001]',limit:'The fixture proves properties only of these explicit operations. Safe production reruns also need source/version, storage and orchestration guarantees.',mistakes:['Repeated unit conversion can corrupt already-converted outputs.','Idempotent code can still encode the wrong business rule.'],takeaways:['Test rerun behavior and invariant preservation.','Separate validation from controlled publication of cleaned outputs.'],practice:[
['What does idempotence mean?','A stable second result','Every input becomes zero','Every output is accurate','Every job runs only once','For a transformation T, idempotence means T(T(x))=T(x) under its domain.'],
['Is dividing by 1000 repeatedly an idempotent unit conversion?','No','Yes universally','Only if values are positive','Only in pandas','Each division rescales the value again, so rerunning on converted output changes it further.'],
['Does idempotence prove semantic correctness?','No','Yes by definition','Only for strings','Only for empty data','A stable transformation may still collapse valid identifiers or implement the wrong rule.'],
['A job is rerun after interruption. What input practice helps recovery?','Immutable versioned snapshots','Only the newest mixed output','Only manually edited rows','Only random samples','Known source states and rule versions make rerun effects reproducible and auditable.'],
['A cleaner passes ordinary rows but fails an empty batch. What should be added?','An explicit boundary-case test','Only a larger dataset','Only a renamed function','Only more logging of private rows','Empty and all-missing cases reveal assumptions hidden by typical examples.'],
['A batch violates a key invariant. What is appropriate?','Stop/quarantine under policy','Disable the invariant','Publish and hide the error','Invent replacement keys','A failed safeguard should trigger reviewed handling rather than be weakened for convenience.'],
['A process writes half the output then crashes. What needs design attention?','The output commit boundary','Only text normalization','Only row sort order','Only chart titles','Safe publication/storage semantics are separate from whether individual cleaning functions are correct.']
],formal:[
['Which equation expresses idempotence?','T(T(x))=T(x)','T(x)=x for all x','T(x)=0 always','T(x)+T(x)=x','An idempotent transformation reaches a fixed result after one application.'],
['Which is a useful pipeline invariant?','Expected key uniqueness','The preferred revenue result','Only a file name','Always fewer rows','An invariant should express a required data relationship rather than force an attractive analytical outcome.'],
['What should an all-missing-column test examine?','Handling and diagnostics','A guaranteed real mean','Automatic causal evidence','Only the header color','A robust policy must specify what happens when there are no observed values to estimate or validate.'],
['A recurring job treats clean dollars as raw cents on its second run. What failed?','Representation tracking','Only the count formula','Only output type hints','Only file sorting','The pipeline must know which source units and transformation versions it is applying.'],
['A normalization rule is idempotent but merges case-sensitive ids. What is wrong?','The semantic rule','The idempotence equation','The existence of strings','The ability to rerun','Rerun stability does not justify an identity-destroying transformation.'],
['A team caches a previous validation pass after changing the rule. What is required?','Input/rule-bound validity','Only the same course name','Only a recent timestamp','Only matching row count','Reusable evidence must bind the actual source and transformation/check definitions, not a loose identifier.'],
['A pipeline accepts duplicate keys only when the total looks correct. What is missing?','Relationship checks','Only a larger sum type','Only more rounding','Only chart labels','Aggregate totals can conceal compensating errors; key contracts should not depend on a preferred total.'],
['A failed job restarts from partially written output without metadata. What risk arises?','Repeated or mixed transformations','Guaranteed exact recovery','Automatic source completeness','Only slower execution','Without clear state boundaries, a rerun can reapply rules or combine incomplete states inconsistently.'],
['A synthetic test passes every fixture. What remains beyond that evidence?','Untested production assumptions','The tested expected outputs','The fact that code executed','That examples were synthetic','Fixtures verify stated cases, not all storage, distributed, source-quality or organizational conditions.'],
['A cleaner is ready technically but its business rules lack approval. What is the right status?','Validated draft pending review','Human-approved automatically','Published by default','A completed factual audit','Execution and contract checks do not substitute for authorized substantive policy review.']
]}
];
module.exports=course('data-cleaning',specs,['missing_data','text','timeseries','duplicates','merging']);
